          "type": "thinking",
          "thinking": "Now I understand the issue. Let me analyze the `cmd_check_anchors` function. The bug is that when a Sigstore attestation exists for a range head (e.g., `anchors/000002.sigstore.json`), the code checks that the attestation is valid, but it doesn't verify that the corresponding `.ots` file (e.g., `anchors/000002.ots`) exists.\n\nLooking at the code in `cmd_check_anchors`:\n\n1. It iterates through all anchor files in the anchors directory.\n2. For each `.sigstore.json` file, it verifies the attestation and adds the range to `ranges`.\n3. For each `.ots` file, it verifies the proof.\n4. At the end, it checks that the ranges cover all entries.\n\nThe problem is that if there's a `.sigstore.json` file for sequence N, but the corresponding `.ots` file is missing, the code doesn't report this as an error. The `.ots` file is only checked if it exists.\n\nAccording to the bug report:\n- `check-anchors` must exit 1 when the `.ots` proof for any attested range head is missing.\n- It must print a message to stderr containing `NNNNNN.ots is missing` (for example `000002.ots is missing`).\n- With the proof present, the existing behaviour must not change.\n\nSo I need to:\n1. After collecting all the ranges from Sigstore attestations, check that for each range head (the `hi` value in each range), there's a corresponding `.ots` file.\n2. If missing, add to `bad` list with the message `NNNNNN.ots is missing`.\n\nLet me look at the code more carefully to see where to add this check.\n\nThe `ranges` list contains tuples of `(from_seq, to_seq, commit)`. The `to_seq` is the range head. For each range head, there should be a corresponding `.ots` file.\n\nI should add this check after the loop that processes all anchor files, but before the coverage check.\n\nLet me write the fix.",
