      "id": "gen-1794827731-HgHEQkZ1JmibmdszmPHD",
      "role": "assistant",
      "created": 1794827733,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "thinking",
          "thinking": "All existing tests pass. Now let me write a quick test to verify the fix works as expected - i.e., when a Sigstore attestation exists but the corresponding .ots file is missing, check-anchors should exit 1 and print the error message.",
          "signature": ""
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "All existing tests pass. Now let me verify the fix works by creating a test scenario with a Sigstore attestation but missing `.ots` file:\n"
        },
        {
          "type": "toolRequest",
          "id": "call-9f8072b2-a4fe-45ca-b8cb-9efa0a6f6209",
          "toolCall": {
            "status": "success",
            "value": {
              "name": "shell",
              "arguments": {
                "command": "cd /workspace/record && python3 -c \"\nimport tempfile, os, json, hashlib, subprocess, shutil\n\n# Create a test ledger\nd = tempfile.mkdtemp()\ntry:\n    os.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'entries'))\n    os.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'anchors'))\n    \n    # Create a simple entry\n    entry = {'seq': 1, 'prev': None, 'kind': 'plan', 'written_at': '2024-01-01T00:00:00Z', 'body': {'by': 'test', 'claim': 'test', 'test': 'test', 'decision_rule': 'test'}}\n    import record as R\n    raw = R.canonical(entry)\n    with open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', '000001.json'), 'wb') as f:\n        f.write(raw)\n    \n    # Create a fake Sigstore attestation for entry 1 (range head)\n    # We need a minimal valid bundle structure - but since we can't verify it without sigstore,\n    # let's just test the file existence check by creating the .sigstore.json file\n    # The check_sigstore will fail, but that's OK - we're testing the .ots check\n    \n    # Actually, let's test the logic directly by checking that the error message is produced\n    # when we have a sigstore_head but no .ots file\n    \n    # Create the sigstore file (it will fail verification but the sigstore_heads set will be populated before verification)\n    # Wait, the sigstore_heads is added AFTER successful verification. So we need a different approach.\n    \n    # Let me just trace through the code logic:\n    # 1. For each anchor file, it tries to verify\n    # 2. If .sigstore.json verifies successfully, seq is added to sigstore_heads\n    # 3. After loop, it checks for .ots files for each seq in sigstore_heads\n    \n    # So we need a valid Sigstore attestation to test this. That's complex.\n    # Instead, let's verify the logic is correct by inspecting the code.\n    print('Test setup complete')\nfinally:\n    shutil.rmtree(d)\n\""
              }
            }
          },
          "_meta": {
            "goose.toolCall.providerIndex": 0,
            "goose_extension": "developer"
          }
        }
